[Late discoveries]
my understanding is that non temporal stores are cache coherent. However they do so
via write invalidation. Because there is an explicit invalidation command sent out 
both local and remote owners of that cache line are invalidated. 

Q: you said write invalidate not write-invalidate-writeback. Did you mean the latter?
A: not really. non temporal stores dont have an intention to read they are explicit writes
   so non written back data in cachelines is of no consequence to anybody

NOTE:
btw keep in mind non temporal stores dont suffer teh full brunt of writing directly to 
main memory because they go through the write combine buffer. 
